### Project Reflection

One of the main design decisions I made in this project was to use Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Memory to keep customer information across different sessions. I wanted the agent to do more than just answer individual questions. By using the customer ID as the actor and retrieving information from the memory namespaces, the agent can remember details such as a customer’s name and communication preferences even when they start a new session. I found this useful because it makes the customer support experience more consistent.

One of the biggest challenges I encountered was getting the different AgentCore components to work together correctly. In particular, I had issues with the runtime payload format and memory retrieval. The runtime was wrapping the request inside the `prompt` field, which initially caused the customer ID to be handled incorrectly. I traced the request structure, adjusted the entrypoint to correctly parse the nested payload, and then tested the memory using two separate sessions with the same customer ID. The second session was successfully able to retrieve the information saved from the first session.

For a production environment, I would focus heavily on security, monitoring, and reliability. I would use stricter IAM permissions for the Gateway, Knowledge Base, Memory, and runtime instead of granting broad access. I would also add structured logging and CloudWatch alarms so that failures can be detected quickly. For a larger customer base, I would consider response validation, better error handling, and conversation summarization to control token usage and cost while preserving important customer context.
